00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:28.370
So I think, you know, knowing that this is
such a huge controversy in a lot of

00:47:28.370 --> 00:47:29.194
denominations,

00:47:29.690 --> 00:47:33.808
especially, I don't know if you have
friends that are not Pentecostal,

00:47:34.090 --> 00:47:38.550
since this can be the biggest hang -up for
people, is the idea of the baptism of the

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:44.610
Holy Spirit. But to remember that above
anything else that we can be unified on,

00:47:44.690 --> 00:47:49.190
that the baptism of the Holy Spirit is
ultimately about power to reach people for

00:47:49.190 --> 00:47:49.890
Jesus.

00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:55.690
And, you know, that it can be, you know,
for some people, disagreements.

00:47:55.910 --> 00:48:01.770
I've had a lot of debates and discussions,
and I love to discuss theology and just to

00:48:01.770 --> 00:48:06.010
talk about it, and to just dive into it
and find scripture and try and figure out

00:48:06.010 --> 00:48:11.000
the meaning of and really just chew on it,
you know, and learn from each other.

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:12.520
But,

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:16.530
and believe it or not, this one has come
up so many times with different people

00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:18.050
that I've talked to.

00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:21.430
But I think ultimately we need to
remember, if you ever come into

00:48:21.430 --> 00:48:25.510
conversation with anyone about the Holy
Spirit, to remember that ultimately it is

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:28.150
about the power to reach people for Jesus,
Jesus to reach people for Jesus.

00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:31.950
So we can kind of move past some of the
disagreements that

00:48:32.930 --> 00:48:33.630
may happen.

00:48:35.860 --> 00:48:38.230
Any other, other thoughts before we move
on?

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:38.990
Okay,

00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:47.110
we see here in verse eight, it says that
Paul entered the synagogue and spoke

00:48:47.110 --> 00:48:51.830
boldly there for three months, arguing
persuasively about the kingdom of God.

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:55.590
And what we see here that,

00:48:56.830 --> 00:49:00.450
it also says here, further on, it says, so
Paul, he, he took the disciples with him

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:04.230
and had discussions daily in the lecture
hall of Tyranus.

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:06.070
Now the lecture hall,

00:49:06.750 --> 00:49:11.770
now we see Paul's method of teaching here,
that he teaches through discussion.

00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:15.670
And I think this is a very key, the book
talks about this, I believe in it

00:49:15.670 --> 00:49:18.317
wholeheartedly, the importance of
discussion.

00:49:18.510 --> 00:49:22.190
And instead of teaching with the lecture,
to teach with discussion.

00:49:22.190 --> 00:49:27.980
And Paul gives this example, he did talk
and teach in the synagogue,

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:31.380
but he moved it, it says, to the lecture
hall.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:35.565
This place, this lecture hall in Tyranus
is famous for lectures.

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:40.155
It was a building where one person would
sit up and did all the talking.

00:49:40.300 --> 00:49:44.940
And you would say that most education
today is a very similar thing.

00:49:45.120 --> 00:49:49.720
Even if you look at going to a church
service, it's a speaker or a pastor who's

00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:51.485
preaching at the congregation.

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:55.960
But we see here that Paul did it, a little
bit differently, where he had discussions

00:49:55.960 --> 00:50:02.040
daily with the people. And he did not fill
up the most hours with lecture, but

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:03.628
instead he led discussions.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:10.650
And some important things to know about
that tactic of teaching, is

00:50:10.650 --> 00:50:15.190
that, if you can imagine, just like we've
talked about already in this, you can take

00:50:15.190 --> 00:50:17.190
the book and it's so meaty, right?

00:50:17.210 --> 00:50:18.622
There's so much content.

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:21.370
We like to fill books with content.

00:50:21.630 --> 00:50:24.170
And there's kind of connotations for
authors.

00:50:24.170 --> 00:50:25.770
The more content you have,

00:50:26.750 --> 00:50:30.450
the smarter you are, or the more in depth
that class is.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:33.670
And it's kind of like a sense of pride,
you know, if you have a textbook that's so

00:50:33.670 --> 00:50:36.570
hard and you talk about your friends in
classes, right?

00:50:36.570 --> 00:50:38.230
It's kind of like this excitement.

00:50:38.230 --> 00:50:43.370
That's a Western tendency to be able to,
we want lots of facts and details.

00:50:44.090 --> 00:50:44.972
But discussion,

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:48.234
less, less content and more discussion.

00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:50.320
Because discussion takes time.

00:50:50.920 --> 00:50:56.420
But what's important to know about
discussion versus lecture, is that you can

00:50:56.420 --> 00:50:59.240
imagine what is it that you tend to
remember more?

00:50:59.240 --> 00:50:59.960
Do

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:07.680
you remember and memorize words that you
read or that you heard?

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:15.200
Usually your memorization is tied to, but
they say it's tied to a relationship,

00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:19.780
right? It's tied to, maybe it's you really
admire that professor, if you're just

00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:23.720
listening. Or it's because you had a
discussion and someone challenged you.

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:26.440
Or you learned something you didn't know
before.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:28.852
You tend to remember things and put value

00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:34.940
when you're with people that you either
admire or you're in a discussion with.

00:51:35.900 --> 00:51:39.860
And so people don't really grow by
memorizing, but you grow by thinking and

00:51:39.860 --> 00:51:46.170
talking, right? you grow by focusing on
something and really trying to understand

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:49.330
it, asking questions and processing
things.

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:50.396
Yeah, Jenny.

00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:54.260
So I just had a point to make on that.

00:51:54.260 --> 00:51:57.220
I had an uncle that,

00:51:57.820 --> 00:52:01.220
and this hit me also in this reading this
week,

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:06.680
and he died a horrible death and he lived
a horrible life.

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:10.900
He was just an out of control alcoholic.

00:52:11.640 --> 00:52:13.200
His wife hated him.

00:52:20.018 --> 00:52:23.665
at their house, when you would go to their
house, it was this.

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:28.280
He was in an oppressive, dark place,

00:52:29.140 --> 00:52:34.520
but this man knew the scripture inside
out.

00:52:34.700 --> 00:52:36.720
I mean, he memorized the scripture.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:42.970
He knew it. He could quote scripture in
King James version, like no one

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:47.840
I had ever known. And I just remember
always thinking,

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:49.610
how in the world

00:52:50.390 --> 00:52:52.650
can he know so much scripture

00:52:54.610 --> 00:53:00.370
of it in him and be so miserable and and
and die so miserably and live so

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:03.140
miserably and not have any so kind much of
it in him and be so miserable and die so

00:53:03.140 --> 00:53:03.440
miserably and live so miserably and not
have any kind of impact on the world

00:53:03.440 --> 00:53:09.690
around him. And when I read that to this
week and the actually

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:14.130
not in the scriptures, but in the
readings, and it talked about memorization

00:53:14.130 --> 00:53:16.680
and how it,

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:22.580
man, that hit me so hard because my whole
life, I've always wondered about my uncle

00:53:22.580 --> 00:53:27.120
and how he could have been like he was and
known the scripture he knew.

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:32.920
And, and he did have it memorized, but he
definitely did not live it.

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:37.480
So, yeah, that's good. And you know, this
memorizing is important.

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:42.160
We want to memorize God's word because
memorizing, knowing God and here's the

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:46.360
different stuff, not memorizing God's
word, those words in our life, knowing

00:53:46.360 --> 00:53:50.320
God's word. And the truth is they say the
statement you can memorize without

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:54.720
understanding. And so understanding God's
word is important because then.

00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:56.140
We can apply it in our lives.

00:53:56.140 --> 00:53:58.260
We can apply it to, you know, like heaven.

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:03.030
You were just sharing about how you felt
moved and how, I mean, actually happening

00:54:03.030 --> 00:54:04.730
in your own life on Saturday.

00:54:04.730 --> 00:54:08.270
And we can apply things as we understand
them.

00:54:08.690 --> 00:54:14.170
But sadly, it's very legalistic view of
Christianity, you just memorize.

00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:19.910
That's kind of the downside to a lot of
Catholic churches is that now, I believe

00:54:19.910 --> 00:54:25.670
there are people who are safe, but for the
most part part is about

00:54:25.670 --> 00:54:28.490
memorizing. It's about knowing the
prayers.

00:54:28.690 --> 00:54:33.770
It's about knowing, you know, knowing, but
not understanding Jesus.

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:38.890
And you know, so many Catholic people who
don't truly have a relationship with Jesus

00:54:38.890 --> 00:54:41.690
because they don't truly understand Jesus.

00:54:41.690 --> 00:54:46.510
And so, but yes, you know, our, that's a
good point,

00:54:47.590 --> 00:54:52.250
Jenny, that to understand, you know, and I
think that's important, even as we

00:54:52.250 --> 00:54:54.150
continue to learn God's word.

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:57.070
A lot of times people talk about when
you're doing your devotions, it's not

00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:01.580
about how much you read, but it's about
what you get from your reading.

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:07.000
Even if all you read that day is one verse
and you're just focusing on one verse and

00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:09.120
you're trying to understand and unpack
that one verse.

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:11.620
What is God saying to me today about this
verse?

00:55:11.620 --> 00:55:13.680
It's not about what you read.

00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:20.330
It's one of the reasons I do not like
daily or the reading through the Bible in

00:55:20.330 --> 00:55:21.030
one year.

00:55:21.570 --> 00:55:25.099
Now, I think that that's great to be able
to read the Bible.

00:55:25.170 --> 00:55:28.950
And I think we should do things where we
try and read the whole Bible.

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:34.390
I think that's great. I have done those
growing up so many times in different

00:55:34.390 --> 00:55:37.590
Bible studies, different groups to be able
to read through the whole Bible in a year.

00:55:37.590 --> 00:55:38.850
I don't think that's a bad thing.

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:43.090
I think that when you're trying to tick
off on your day's calendar, I've read

00:55:43.090 --> 00:55:47.210
these chapters. You're not always really
diving in and understanding what you're

00:55:47.210 --> 00:55:50.150
reading. you're just reading so that you
can mark it off.

00:55:50.150 --> 00:55:51.730
Okay, I read my chapter today.

00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:56.330
So I think it's important to remember as
we're following Jesus and as we're reading

00:55:56.330 --> 00:56:00.310
the word and as you disciple people and as
you become an influence in someone else's

00:56:00.310 --> 00:56:04.630
life, to remember that ultimately
understand the word of God, not memorizing

00:56:04.630 --> 00:56:05.571
the word of God.

00:56:06.050 --> 00:56:10.790
And I think, you know, another in the
Middle Eastern culture, it's very much on

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:15.550
this next point, they make the power in
the name of Jesus flows through believers.

00:56:15.550 --> 00:56:17.430
Power does not flow through words.

00:56:17.430 --> 00:56:19.550
It flows through relationships.

00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:24.720
In the Middle Eastern culture, you will
see it's all about...

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:27.018
Can I say something? Yeah, go ahead.

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:36.420
I just, I was glad that you pointed out
something about discussion because because

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:42.490
Jasper and I have actually spent a bit of
time in Jewish

00:56:42.490 --> 00:56:48.750
synagogues and attending a Jewish
discussion of a Torah

00:56:48.750 --> 00:56:49.450
passage

00:56:50.170 --> 00:56:55.220
is like a discussion that I have never
experienced anywhere else.

00:56:56.900 --> 00:56:58.380
Because as

00:56:59.170 --> 00:57:03.950
Christians, we tend to, and I think it's
part of Western Greek

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:09.130
thinking, we always think you have to
arrive at the right answer.

00:57:09.750 --> 00:57:16.090
But a Jewish discussion was more like
every person contributing

00:57:16.090 --> 00:57:18.850
what they thought the verse meant.

00:57:19.870 --> 00:57:23.850
And in leaving the group, it was more
about...

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:30.250
That was a great view you shared.

00:57:30.410 --> 00:57:36.410
I never thought about it that way, but it
wasn't about, okay, everyone get the final

00:57:36.410 --> 00:57:42.850
right answer. And so when you brought up
discussion, that's the

00:57:42.850 --> 00:57:47.140
picture that came to mind and I like what
you just said, that it's about

00:57:47.140 --> 00:57:50.000
relationship and that was the focus.

00:57:51.100 --> 00:57:55.060
It's not that they were trying to teach
wrong things,

00:57:55.720 --> 00:58:01.200
but the focus was relationship more than
does everybody have the right answer now,

00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:03.360
okay, we can go home. that came to

00:58:04.940 --> 00:58:08.480
Yeah, no, it's good. It's a great point
that they make.

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:13.320
And I do admire that about Middle Eastern
culture,

00:58:14.180 --> 00:58:15.298
the Jewish culture,

00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:16.859
the Muslim culture

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:19.500
is very much a discussion.

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:24.540
And if you were to minister to the Jews,
if you're going to minister to the

00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:27.340
Muslims, it is a lot of discussion -based.

00:58:27.340 --> 00:58:32.540
It is a lot of sitting down and just
looking at scripture and understanding it.

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:37.200
And yeah, a lot of people have different
opinions.

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:39.400
That's a great point.

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:42.720
I have myself not been to Israel yet.

00:58:42.900 --> 00:58:46.360
Someday I hope to. But I've heard a lot of
people and a lot of people talk about that

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:49.900
is something that has made such an impact
on their life is going into a synagogue

00:58:49.900 --> 00:58:51.900
and being a part of the discussion

00:58:52.300 --> 00:58:55.000
because you're able to look at the Bible a
different way.

00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:58.160
I don't know if you guys have ever been to
a really good small group, some small

00:58:58.160 --> 00:59:01.631
groups are like that where I have a small
group that I love

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:05.900
and it is where we take one chapter a
night.

00:59:06.340 --> 00:59:09.105
Sometimes it ends up just being half a
chapter,

00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:11.680
but you just take a section of scripture.

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:16.800
We all read it and then we just think
about it and then we talk about, well,

00:59:16.860 --> 00:59:18.360
what does this mean to you?

00:59:18.360 --> 00:59:20.720
What stood out to you about this
scripture?

00:59:20.720 --> 00:59:21.940
And everyone discusses it.

00:59:21.940 --> 00:59:23.560
And then we have discussions.

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:25.913
The rest of the Bible study is all about

00:59:25.960 --> 00:59:28.666
what different people got from that
scripture.

00:59:29.200 --> 00:59:31.100
And I always feel like I learn.

00:59:31.300 --> 00:59:32.420
I feel like I'm challenged.

00:59:32.420 --> 00:59:35.920
I feel like I grow so much when I look at
it that way.

00:59:38.100 --> 00:59:38.806
That's good.

00:59:43.260 --> 00:59:44.084
The other last

00:59:44.900 --> 00:59:49.547
section up here. Anyone else actually want
to share anything before we move on?

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:55.956
No? Okay, we'll go ahead and move on.

00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:02.020
And we talked about when it comes to the
drachmas,

01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:03.549
talks about these scrolls.

01:00:03.920 --> 01:00:07.260
We see here in the scripture that
believers should make a public break with

01:00:07.260 --> 01:00:12.160
past sins. There was a huge, I don't know
if you guys ever remember this, but

01:00:12.370 --> 01:00:16.570
several years ago, I was quite young
during the time, but it was like a the big

01:00:16.570 --> 01:00:19.370
thing to like bring your sins to the
altar.

01:00:20.570 --> 01:00:26.410
So it was like CDs of like like music or
movies or books or whatever it is.

01:00:26.510 --> 01:00:29.350
You bring it to the altar and people would
often burn it.

01:00:29.350 --> 01:00:33.270
It was like this huge part of revival
nights.

01:00:33.690 --> 01:00:38.830
And we don't do it very much anymore in
the church but we see that here.

01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:41.230
And it's not so much about bringing it to
the altar.

01:00:41.230 --> 01:00:43.230
It's not so much about burning it.

01:00:43.250 --> 01:00:48.230
It's more about making a choice, a public
choice to break with your past sin.

01:00:48.830 --> 01:00:52.536
The way that we kind of do that today is
through water baptism.

01:00:52.670 --> 01:00:57.690
I would say is more the way that the
church does this idea of breaking with

01:00:57.690 --> 01:00:58.770
your past sin.

01:00:59.370 --> 01:01:02.017
Publicly saying I'm choosing to follow
Jesus.

01:01:02.330 --> 01:01:07.370
I'm choosing to try and have a Christ
-like behavior in my life.

01:01:08.070 --> 01:01:09.790
And so why did they burn it though here?

01:01:09.790 --> 01:01:10.910
What was the importance?

01:01:10.910 --> 01:01:11.990
We see that they,

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:16.740
because they burned them publicly.

01:01:17.860 --> 01:01:21.360
And then the Catholic, it's interesting
that they calculated the value of the

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:26.900
scrolls and it came to 50 ,000 drachmas or
50 ,000

01:01:28.130 --> 01:01:29.610
days' labor.

01:01:30.750 --> 01:01:36.110
And, but what I think that the value
showing that is even though this was a

01:01:36.110 --> 01:01:42.510
huge value, it was witchcraft books or
scrolls that we can say as books that they

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:43.922
brought and they burned.

01:01:44.390 --> 01:01:47.990
And this, as you can imagine, in Ephesus,
we've talked about this before, it's a

01:01:47.990 --> 01:01:50.710
very dark city. It's a very dark region.

01:01:50.710 --> 01:01:55.690
That's all about witchcraft, all about
paganism, all about Artemis and fertility

01:01:55.690 --> 01:01:59.670
and sex and evilness is all a part of the
city.

01:02:00.380 --> 01:02:01.220
And so,

01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:07.320
for them to bring their scrolls together
and burn them publicly,

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:10.560
they're not selling them, right?

01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:12.340
They're not trying to get a profit from
it.

01:02:12.340 --> 01:02:15.360
Instead, they're completely breaking from
the sin.

01:02:16.200 --> 01:02:16.900
So,

01:02:18.440 --> 01:02:22.680
the other reason doing it publicly is that
we know that sin grows in dark places,

01:02:22.680 --> 01:02:26.980
right? When things aren't publicly brought
forward, it's harder to break from that

01:02:26.980 --> 01:02:29.220
sin because no one, one else knows about
it.

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:32.400
It can fester and it can hurt our lives.

01:02:32.580 --> 01:02:35.168
And so, the importance of doing it
publicly.

01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:39.120
Now, today, we don't have to have the
whole full revival and people bring their

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:43.400
sins and, burn them at the altar but ways
to encourage as you are going to cycle

01:02:43.400 --> 01:02:44.480
others

01:02:46.400 --> 01:02:51.720
to talk about these things, maybe with a
mentor or if you are mentoring someone, to

01:02:51.720 --> 01:02:55.940
be able to have a place of accountability
where you can talk about struggles, you

01:02:55.940 --> 01:02:59.000
can talk about frustrations and be able
to,

01:02:59.860 --> 01:03:03.540
you know, if you were ever mentoring
someone, it may be really uncomfortable,

01:03:03.540 --> 01:03:07.128
but to ask people, how are you doing in
your walk with Jesus?

01:03:07.400 --> 01:03:12.220
You know, how are you doing on some of the
struggles and temptations that you had?

01:03:12.220 --> 01:03:17.790
Why? Because you're letting them air out
and publicly share things that they're

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:21.990
